zation of reticular materials remains challenging. Strong chemical bonding between building units and the formation of competing phases result in a narrow crystallization window that requires precise control over reaction parameters. In the absence of a reliable theoretical framework for predicting crystallization outcomes, synthesis remains largely trial-and-error and dependent on human intuition. To address this limitation, this dissertation introduces an autonomous platform, termed Algorithmic Iterative Reticular Synthesis (AIRES), which integrates high-throughput experimentation and machine learning to identify correlations between successful crystallization outcomes, building block properties, and reaction parameters and therefore to accelerate the discovery of new reticular materials. Chapter 1 introduces the conceptual framework of autonomous platforms for chemistry and materials science, w

